  "id": "Compound::DB08816 - Disease::DOID:3393",
  "verbalization": "Ticagrelor, a P2Y12 Receptor Antagonist, is linked to Coronary Artery Disease through its role in decreasing platelet aggregation, as evidenced by its inclusion in the category of Decreased Platelet Aggregation. This mechanism is supported by its relationship with Clopidogrel, a well-known P2Y12 inhibitor, which in turn is associated with the treatment of Coronary Artery Disease. The shared lowest common ancestor of Ticagrelor and Clopidogrel, both belonging to the class of Organonitrogen Heterocyclic Compounds and P2Y12 Inhibitors, underscores a common pharmacological pathway. While the direct causal link between Ticagrelor and Coronary Artery Disease is not explicitly stated, the indirect pathway through Clopidogrel provides a plausible mechanism. The evidence suggests that Ticagrelor may contribute to the management of Coronary Artery Disease via its antiplatelet effects, aligning with the broader pharmacological profile of P2Y12 inhibitors.",
